    {
      "id": "ef7w6fu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "FUNDAMENTAL DUTIES"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Fundamental Duties were incorporated into Part IV-A of the Constitution under Article 51A through the 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1976.",
        "The concept was inspired by the Constitution of the erstwhile USSR and recommended by the Sardar Swaran Singh Committee.",
        "Originally 10 in number, an 11th Fundamental Duty was added by the 86th Constitutional Amendment Act in 2002.",
        "Fundamental Duties are non-justiciable in nature and apply exclusively to citizens of India, not to foreigners.",
        "Parliament has the legal authority to enforce these duties by enacting suitable legislation."
      ],
      "id": "bgl71ee"
    },
